        <h2 id="deep-dive">The 3 Biggest Mechanical Differences</h2>

        <h3>1. The Living Mist vs. Static Radiation</h3>
        <p>In Zero Sievert, radiation is a geographic puzzle: equip a geiger counter, steer clear of glowing rocks, or pop anti-rad pills. In <strong>Scavland</strong>, <em>The Mist</em> is a dynamic global event. When anomalous fog rolls into the sector, outdoor visibility drops to near-zero, infected mutants become hyper-aggressive, and your gas mask filters begin depleting by the minute. Surviving requires portable anomaly scanners, emergency shelters, and prepared extraction routes.</p>

        <h3>2. Catastrophic Weapon Degradation</h3>
        <p>Gunfights in Scavland carry high mechanical risk. Firing scavenged weapons in poor condition can result in stovepipe jams, failure to eject, or even catastrophic receiver explosions that permanently destroy the firearm and injure the player. You cannot simply sprint into high-tier zones with 15% durability junk guns.</p>

        <h3>3. Handcrafted Environmental Storytelling</h3>
        <p>While Zero Sievert excels at fast-paced procedural roguelike runs, Scavland's surface overworld of <strong>Zalesye</strong> is deliberately handcrafted. Every ruined Soviet radar station, collapsed bridge, and underground bunker entrance has intentional environmental narrative, tactical sightlines, and hidden stashes.</p>

        <h2 id="verdict">The Verdict</h2>
        <p>If you love Zero Sievert's core loop but crave deeper immersion, true weapon building, and the terrifying survival atmosphere of Darkwood and S.T.A.L.K.E.R., <strong>Scavland is the exact natural evolution you have been waiting for</strong>.</p>

